Date: Sat, 19 Jun 93 03:02:52 -0400
From: hhuang@Athena.MIT.EDU
Subject: DIET: fats and fatty acids

I felt that I may as well post some more on these subjects to give a
fuller context.  Besides, I am learning as I write (and making some
changes to my diet and supplementation accordingly).

Correction: as somebody pointed out to me, I used the term
``nootropil'' incorrectly.  Before I sent my last post, I was using
``nootropic,'' which is the proper usage, but then I chickened out and
used nootropil exclusively.  Mea culpa.

In my previous post, I noted that ``alpha-linoleic'' acid was one of
the two essential fatty acids from which other essentials fatty acids
can be made.  The name is actually ``alpha-linolenic'' (it's the
``n,'' stupid :).  Careless, mea culpa.  Also, supposedly ``Alena''
(Alpha linoLENic Acid) is a new coinage of this word.  If what some
sources say is correct, Alena deficiency might be the most widespread
deficiency in America.

This is 1/2 of the essential fatty acid path:

Alena -> EPA -> DHA

The other 1/2 is:

linoleic (note, not linoLENic) acid -> GLA (gamma-linoLENic acid) ->
Dihomogamma-linolenic acid -> arachidonic acid [this is the proper
spelling of the fatty acid said to be found primarily in animal
products]

The name of the prostaglandin that EPA (another fatty acid) helps to
stimulate production of is prostacyclin (I recall it being named PGI2
in another context), which helps to prevent the stickier kinds of
blood cells, hence helping keep the blood vessels free of dangerous
clots.  There was also one reference claiming that diet EPA helps cut
down on production of thromboxane, which is also a prostaglandin, but
one that induces blood cell clumping.  It seems that EPA and
arachidonic acid are at cross purposes when it comes to clot
formations.  So EPA helps against platelet aggregation and also thins
the blood as well: taking too much, as one enthusiastic Oxford
professor did when eating an almost all fish diet, resulted in the
Oxford professor developing anemia and dangerously prolonged bleeding
times.

Also, I found very interesting a study where lactating mothers taking
fish oil supplements, which contain EPA and DHA, had dramatic
increases in the DHA in their milk.  DHA is found heavily in the
brain, the retina.  Note that these polyunsaturated fats are very
easily oxidized, and can go dangerously rancid very quickly.  In fact,
there are some experiments which show that rats being fed diets with
polyunsaturated fats (uncertain whether they were being fed
unprocessed oils or processed oils, which have can be in the trans-
formation; this is very important; more below), that the higher the
level of polyunsatured fats, the more errors they made in maze tests.
Talk about neg-nootropic effects!  In fact, the fact that the brain is
so highly made of polyunsatured fats explains, for example, the
vitamin C pumps that keep brain levels of vitamin C far above blood
vitamin C levels, because the vitamin C is an anti-oxidant that
protects the easily peroxidized brain tissue.

Also, diets high in EPA and DHA can significantly reduce the amount of
bodily leukotrienes, which cause inflammation of tissue.  Some studies
on arthritis show noticeable improvement with fish oil supplements
with respect to placebo groups.  Some sources also cite that GLA,
found in evening primrose oil, may help arthritis as well.

The best source of linoleic and linolenic acids appears to be flax or
linseed oil.  These acids, particularly GLA, Alena, and linoleic acid,
form the top of the fatty acid chain I typed out above.  You know,
it's a shame that with many health supplements and foods, that there
will be people making irresponsible and often downright fraudulent
claims on a certain food, which then leads to a backlash from an
obsessive-compulsive cure-over-prevention medical establishment (by
the way, how many doctors do you see actually practicing what they
preach?; how many doctors aren't overweight?; hmm...).  For example,
the fascist FDA (I presume it's the FDA; who else has the jurisdiction
to ban foodstuffs?) at one point put restrictions upon linseed oil
because of all the claims its promoters were making.  But you can
purchase linseed oil now.  An interesting snippet I picked up was that
cooking with linseed oil can be pretty pungent, since it might smell a
lot like fish being fried.

One source says that one ought to get all one's EPA and DHA not from
fish oils or from fish, but rather from linseed or other plant oils,
because not only are plant oils cheaper, less easy to peroxidize, but
it may be better to let the body decide how much EPA and DHA it needs
and convert from Alena to EPA and DHA on its own.  Also, some people,
older people, it seems, can't convert linoleic acid to GLA well due to
a decline in the requisite enzyme delta-5-desaturase, so evening
primrose oil would be recommended as well.

Beware that the most essential fats are these polyunsatured fats,
which are heavily prone to oxidation.  Linseed oil, for example, needs
to be in black bottles (dark brown doesn't cut it) and needs to be
refrigerated to prevent oxidation, and bottles should have a pressing
date and four month expiration date.  Sheesh; you may as well put some
ascorbyl palmitate or BHA or BHT while you are at it, just to be safe.

A very important word of caution.  All sources have remarked upon how
the cis- form of fatty acids is what is found in nature, and that
trans- forms, which are produced when oils and fats are processed, are
dangerous and should be eliminated from the diet.  Thus, when one buys
these oils, one needs to make sure that they are cold-pressed and
unrefined.  Bleaching, deodorizing, hydrogenation, and heating, all
these will ruin the cis- form fats and turn them into the dangerous
trans- form.  I've heard too much about all the bad things of trans-
form polyunsatured fats.  For example, that they inhibit the liver's
ability to turn fats into other useful substances and fats, help raise
blood fat levels, and the fact that your body is building itself with
fats that are just plain unnatural and serve no biological purpose
except to interfere in normal bodily functions.

So ironically, margarine and other hydrogenated fats, such as
shortening, seem to do more harm than the animal saturated fats,
butter for example, that they were meant to replace.  A figure was
given that 60% of fats consumed in the American diet are hydrogenated.
Add to that the fact that Americans also eat too much saturated fats.
Saturated fats seem to do little than be burned for energy.  In other
words, dietary saturated fats seem to go primarily into right into
one's bodily fat stores; or rather, that a gram of saturated fat is
probably going to wind up as a gram of unwanted cellulose and beer
bellies.  There is other research, which I am less familiar with, that
has led many public sources to declaim against saturated fats because
they help cause heart disease.  The effects of saturated fats seem to
be as they are muchly because, as you can tell by now, polyunsatured
fats have many double bonds which are the reason for why they are so
easily oxidized as well as being used for the infinite biological
purposes in your body.  Saturated fats, on the contrary, saturate all
the double bonds with hydrogen.  Note that monosaturated means that
there is one double bond, and poly means, of course, many.  Finally,
your body seems to be able to make the saturated fats that it needs
(and it does need them to protect your organs and pad your skin, just
as in animals) from unsaturated fats.  Hence, saturated fats are
nonessential, and everywhere people say that one should eliminate or
radically cut down on one's intake of them.

Thus, the majority of fats in the American diet are either downright
toxic (hydrogenated fats) or plain fattening (saturated fats), both
types being contributors to clogged arteries and heart disease.  No
wonder it seems that many come to the conclusion that fat is all bad
-- because practically all sources of fat in the American diet are
bad.  Ironically, taking supplements of certain ``good'' fats will
help *undo* the life-endangering effects of the ``bad'' fats.  The
effects can be dramatic.  The whole interest in EPA began with
noticing that Eskimos ate little fiber (not too many plants growing up
there, ya know), lots of fat and protein, but still had low levels of
heart disease, and if anything a prolonged bleeding time.  By the
standard brain-dead ``common wisdom,'' those Eskimos should all be
dying in high proportions of heart disease.  But such is not the case.
More rants on my perceptions of massive public disinformation and
confusion on health matters later.

I'd like to just quote (without permission) from pages 86-87 of
Michael Colgan's _Optimum Sports Nutrition_, which can be recommended
for one no nonsense no hype scientific source of sports nutrition (and
what's good for an athlete's body is in general good for *your* body,
too; don't let the title mislead you, and you *do* intend to exercise
regularly, *don't* you?  :),

``Hardly and trans fatty acids occur in Nature, so the human body has
never developed the mechanisms necessary to use them.  Today, almost
all processed fat and oil products, cooking oils, margarines, and fats
used in breads, cookies, candies, chocolates, frozen dinners, pies,
and processed meats, contain high levels of nutritionally damaging
trans fatty acids.  If you want a premium, *eliminate all processed
oils from your diet* [emphasis author's].''

Another book, Ross Pelton's _Mind Food and Smart Pills_ has on pages
262-263, which I quote from without permission,

``It is the change in the shape of the molecule that makes trans fats
toxic.  If this is the kind of fat you give to your body and brain,
this is the type of fat from which your cell walls will be made.
However, enzymes that take nutrients in and out of cells cannot
interact with this type of fat structure.  This results in improperly
nourished cells causing toxic buildup within the cells.

``The most commonly used partially hydrogenated products include
margarine, vegetable shortenings, and shortening oils.  It is estimate
that margarine accounts for about 3.5 grams of partially hydrogenated
fats per day in our diets and shortening accounts for another 4.6
grams.  Even if you do not use margarines and shortening in your
kitchen, you would be amazed by the amount of hydrogenated fats in
other products you buy.  Check the labels on cookies, crackers,
breads, baked goods, chips, popcorn, frozen prepared foods, peanut
butter, mixes for baked goods, and salad dressings.  You will need to
start shopping at a good, comprehensive health food store in order to
avoid products containing hydrogenated fats.''

Damn: breads, cookies and other baked goods, frozen dinners, peanut
butter, salad dressings, pies, and processed meats?  That's
practically all the sources of fats that I am currently consuming in
my diet.

Note that no nutrition/supplement book has ever been had all the fully
relevant information on nutrition.  *Every* book, it seems, has missed
something essential to a better understanding of human nutrition.  And
a comment on Pearson and Shaw: they seemed to have been missing the
boat on certain issues lately.  You would think that anybody with that
much money, public visibility, would do exhaustive research with all
the time and resources -- not to mention monetary benefits to be
gained thereby -- at their hands.  This goes for all the nutritional
experts and authors of the books I have been looking through.  Dean
Ornish, for example, has written some excellent books on a whole set
of healthy habits to help prevent heart disease, but he seems to be
missing some of the most interesting findings in recent nutritional
science, particularly regarding supplementation.

So the point is that nobody has ever done a thorough job, and
furthermore, all sources need to be qualified and taken with some
grains of salt.  In addition, many sources will cite sources, or
sources about sources, or hearsay, or hearsay from hearsay.  So, for
example as I am typing this, I brought 5 nutrition/supplement books in
front of me to this workstation, and I am flipping through and pulling
from each one as appropriate (which is why I'm not bothering to give
all the exact individual sources).  So it's difficult to know which
sources to trust.  Go by reputation and intuition, I guess.

I ask a question which has been asked before, ``Is there a
nutritionist in the house?''  Any doctors (whom I may have offended
:)?  Or are we mostly computer science, engineering, and business
folk, as Paul Cisek's survey would seem to show?

So, anyhow, hydrogenated and saturated fats are basically bad for you.
So thus the standards nutrition advice that all unsaturated
non-hydrogenated vegetable oils are basically safe, correct?  Nope!

Oxygen, heat, and light are what damage polyunsaturated seed oils, so
even the best available cold-pressed and unrefined seed oils are still
damaged a bit in the processing.  So the standard industrial scale,
high heat processed, refined, bleach and deodorized, oils are far
worse, with fats damaged enough that they can longer be useful in the
body, and become dangerous to one's health.

So I wonder if the knee-jerking anti-animal fats crowd realized this
when they crowed about how they pressured McDonalds into finally
replacing the fat for their french fries from using animal lard to
using vegetable oils, which have less saturated fats and cholesterol.
Surely, McDonalds is using cold-pressed, unrefined oils, which they
are further very careful to avoid any peroxidation, applying generous
doses of lipid antioxidants, and constantly refrigerating them and
otherwise keeping them out of heat, light, and oxygen.  And of course,
McDonald's oils aren't kept frying around all day, getting peroxidized
with the constant heat and recycled use (such as in the case of their
fries).  And no, the smell from McDonalds and other fast food joints.
This has nothing to do with the fact that using fresh, clean
*processed* (unprocessed is unrefined, and undeodorized, thus will
have a smell) oil doesn't result in anywhere near as much smell.
Yeah, right.  The pomposity of some of these ``health advocate''
groups sometimes just galls me.

One last word, and I am sure there is much more information, as many
experts have spent their life researching fatty acids, Michael Colgan
also writes on page 83,

``Extra virgin [means unprocessed] olive oil, recommended for athletes
for more than a decade by the Colgan Institute, contains high levels
of a monounsaturated fatty acid, called *cis-oleic acid* [emphasis
author's].  Although not essential, repeated studies have shown that
dietary oleic acid lowers serum cholesterol, and has other beneficial
effects on blood lipids. [gives two footnoted references]''

So just to rant on the other rants in the health marketplace and
information centers.  Some sectors say that all fats are bad, and one
should cut down on *all* fats indiscriminantly.  Oh?  What about
certain essential fatty acids, some of which have pronounced
anti-clotting, anti-heart disease, and anti-inflammatory effects?  Or
how about the sectors that rather than advising cutting down on most
bad fats in genral, but instead first recommend substitute unsaturated
fats for saturated fats because of the cholesterol and other blood
lipid levels effects?  Well, gee, what about the dangerous trans- fats
from hydrogenation, or the peroxidation that can result from
unsaturated fats being exposed too much to heat, light, and oxygen?
Peroxidized fats are catalyze *free radicals reactions*, and thus are
not only far worse for your heart than saturated fats, but they will
do other damage as well, such as depressing your immune system,
causing cancer, cross-linking your tissues, and helping to deplete
your sources of bodily antioxidants.  It is true that cholesterol can
be traced to increases in blood concentrations of the killer.  But it
is also true, as Pearson and Shaw write in _Life Extension_, and many
other sources (I am reminded of _The 8-week Cholesterol Cure_), that
your body produces more cholesterol per day than in the average
cholesterol-heavy American diet.  So what gives?  It's obviously a
much more complex process.  Or how about the fact that high doses of
niacin might help cut down on blood cholesterol levels?  So given all
these fuzzy areas, it's really irresponsible of our so-called ``health
experts'' to say that either all fat is bad, or that unsaturated fats
are always better than saturated fats, without giving serious
qualifications to their advice.

Don't take my tone wrong -- I am not attacking or implicating any list
members.  I used to be fed large amounts of this disinformation as
well.  Not only that, I was the one, for example, who got my mother to
substitute margarine for butter, among other things, and was a bit
``science and the experts say so, so there'' about it, too.  But our
so-called experts really ought to know better.  And I have become very
disappointed with the often fascist AMA/FDA medicine establishment.
Furthermore, I am convinced that many of the cure therapies are too
drug-happy, analagous examples being what we see on television all the
time: ``Got a problem with X?  Take super duper Y for what ails you!''
That's right -- pop a pill Y that has who knows how many side effects,
and don't address the possible underlying roots of one's problem X.

It's all very anti-science, anti-reason, anti-reality, really.  ``Just
gimme what works, doc.  I don't have the time or energy to worry about
my health.''  In other words, you don't have the care or will to think
and take proper precautions, you just want whatever works for now.
Side effects, who has the time to worry about them?  Just go to the
doc, and get take some more medicine if there are any complications.
And when you get heart disease or cancer, well, everybody dies in the
end anyway, right?

So enough rants and sloppy summarization (this post) of summarizations
(books, other sources) of summarizations (papers) of fats and
nutrition for now.  There is much more, I am sure, and the science is
fairly recent, too recent for many doctors, who are often overworked
and cannot keep up with the newest research, to keep up with entirely.

To repeat, I am no expert.  Though having taken organic chemistry, I
do not know even biochemistry (though I shall be purchasing some texts
soon), let alone human nutrition and supplements.  If I'm being
mislead, I value the truth more than appearing like a know-it-all who
can never be wrong.

And no, I am not referring to list members when I say ``a know-it-all
who can never be wrong.''  I am referring, rather, to conservative
types, politically as well as in other matters.  Next to
anarcho-capitalist extropy-minded Extropians, practically everybody,
be they political liberals or conservatives, will seem too
``conservative,'' in the proper definition of the word.

I harbor a distaste for a general type of anti-reasoning conservatism
which would seem to place reputation, prestige, power, and all related
auras, above the quest for and reassurance of truth.  It seems that
these types of people forget that ultimately all power and wealth
derive from a mastering of reality somewhere down the line.  Truth can
never follow prestige and power; rather prestige and power must
ultimately follow the truth.

So when I refer to ``know-it-alls,'' I don't feel that the Extropians
list is too bad about this.  I don't mind abrasiveness as much if it
comes with truth, or the best that we can get at the truth.  What I
mind is the abrasiveness of people who are wrong, especially of those
who are intellectually dishonest as well.

What I dislike is not so much flamage, but rather *noise*.  Hence, I
dislike flamage *because* it often leads to noise.  Let's not in our
haste forget that signal is what we are after in the end.

After all, many political arguments are often just densely packed
polemics.  And if they are libertarian/anarcho-capitalist arguments,
then (my bias, of course) I certainly do not feel that many of these
arguments could be considered content free.

Hope to have made myself clearer.

