Subject: DIET: dairy; lecithin; fatty acids; meat; other nutritional comments

Long email on miscellaneous info that I've recently encountered, and a
few rants on supplements, nutrition, nootropics, and so forth.

Last night when I was looking for books at Harvard Square, I stopped
by the Pizzeria Uno's and ordered a ``veggie'' pizza, with double
extra toppings of minced garlic.  My tastes seem to have changed; I
found the pizza too greasy and too salty, and my meal left me with a
mild nausea afterwards.  This has been my same experience lately
whenever I experiment with a grease or protein bomb for meals.  This
is aside from, or perhaps related to, the fact that such meals drain
blood away from the brain and can leave one feeling dopey for hours
afterwards.  Lately, I've also been noticing that the dishes at the
local Chinese restaurants which I used to have enjoyed now seem on the
salty or spicy side for me.  Heh.  Self-transformation, no?

The pizza I ate contained lots of cheese.  And as of this very moment,
I'm sniffling and swallowing noticeably more mucous (2.5+ times) than
I had been when I had kept myself dairy-free for about a week.  When I
had quit the 40 oz. of milk I had been consuming daily, I noticed a
major decrease in mucous production.  The above two cases is
convincing enough.

Finally, I bought some of TwinLab's hyperexpensive PC-55 product,
which is 55% phostatidyl choline.  Though I don't know under what kind
of packaging conditions PC-55 is manufactured under, the label does
say that they put vitamin E (the tocopherol form, I presume, as the
acetate form would protect squat) and ascorbyl palmitate (fat-soluble
vitamin C) to help retard rancidity.  I compared the smell with some
of the store bought lecithin granules that I had bought (granules are
generally around 20-25% phostatidyl choline).  The store bought
granules (the store, by the way, is Nature and Food Centers,
apparently a Boston area outfit) had a smell that stung my nose; the
PC-55, by contrast, didn't.  This is convincing enough for me to be
wary of buying most store bought lecithin in the *granule* form -- the
same recommendation Pearson and Shaw gave in their _Life Extension
Companion_.  I don't know enough to say of the capsule or liquid forms.
I guess I'd have to buy some and sniff them.

Pearson and Shaw said _LEC_ that they might be working on a simple
peroxidization test.  Does anybody know further about this, or of any
other simple peroxidization or free radical tests?  I'd certainly like
to use such a test on the items of food that I can get my hands on.

I phoned the Life Extension Foundation as to how they kept their
lecithin from rancidity, and a woman told me that they do not purchase
their products in large batches which will sit there.  In fact, for
some of us who purchase from LEF, this is why products are often back
ordered: because they ran out of their limited stock.  LEF sells two
types of lecihin: in the granules form, which I worry about, and in a
BHA added powder form.  The woman suggested ordering the granules form
one bottle at a time, despite the four bottles discount, and then
refrigerating the bottle, and then consuming it quickly.  (TwinLab
suggests not refrigerating PC-55, as it will accumulate moisture; does
anybody have more data on which method is preferable?)  The powder
form with BHA, however, would go rancid slower, she said.  My guess is
that the powder form is also slower to go rancid because there is less
surface area for oxygen to interact with the polyunsatured fats.  But
my guess could be wrong, as the rancidity could be mostly catalyzed by
the already existent free radicals.

I also looked at LEF's catalog and saw two ``secondary'' anti-oxidants
preservatives for sale (BHA & BHT, presumably, aren't secondary
anti-oxidants).  In other words, supposedly these not only help
prevent oxidation, but they may also help undo the oxidation that is
already there.  Does anybody have any further information on this?
Perhaps such preservatives would be useful for foods that might be
questionable?

Given that I had been unknowingly consuming a good quantity of
peroxidized lecithin, I've decided to put 10 g of ascorbyl palmitate
per 100 g of PC-55, which is the amount per bottle.  Just to be on the
safe side.  And I think I'll stick with PC-55 for now.  The price of
security is worth it for me.

I'm also getting irritated at Dean and Morgenthaler in how sketchy
they often make things out to be.  For example, they recommend up to 3
grams daily of choline from choline bitartrate and choline chloride.
They also mention that if one is getting one's choline from
phostatidyl choline, that one might want to consume more than 3 grams
because only part of phostatidyl choline is choline.  Like, really!
When I phoned LEF some time back, a woman flipped to some reference
and told me that PC was only 11% choline.  Wow, to get 3 grams of
choline, one would have to take 27 grams of PC per day!  Now I've been
trying choline chloride, and I've found that one doesn't need anywhere
near a 9:1 ratio of PC to choline to get a noticeable effect (my
noticeable are unmistakable, and seem to be more
psychological/physiological than cognitive).  PC is more bioavailable
than plain choline, as can be explained by why some people get
diarrhea or start to stink like a fish with choline, but not with PC.

Also, when D&M say to take lots of vitamin B5, they could kindly
remind us that B5 forms half the molecule of the very enzyme that
converts choline into acetylcholine.  This is why large doses of B5
and choline will cause stomach contractions, because the B5 will
increase ACh in the gut, causing it to contract (all this info is
found in Pearson and Shaw, by the way).  I had been taking lecithin
for a while, and one morning I increased my B5 concentration from 50
mg w/ lecithin & 100 mg DMAE in a morning dose to 250 mg B5 with the
same amounts of lecithin and DMAE.  Whoosh: I had ACh overdose, with
mild stomach cramps, a head that felt full and like it was swimming,
and a runny nose (ACh is supposed to stimulate mucous production as
well, so some say).  It took almost a week before my stomach adjusted
itself to that level of B5 intake.

I'm getting quite irritated at the fact that supplements and
nutritional sources are often directly contradictory (take this, no
take that; that will hurt this, no it won't, yes it does, liar, same
to you and many more; etc.) and that furthermore seem to prefer not
giving the full biochemical details.  Flow charts, even for simple
reactions, have a much more powerful mnemonic and comprehensive effect
than words.  I'm not stupid; I can understand basic high school
biochemistry if it is explained well enough to me.  And we all know
that if we just draw out on paper the mechanisms or general pattern of
something new, that we'd learn it much quicker and fuller.

A good example is about how B6 helps convert tryptophan to serotonin
and how C & B6 helps convert l-phenylalanine and l-tyrosine into
dopamine and norepinephrine.  Great, so whenever I swallow
dl-phenylalanine on an empty stomach, I put C & B6 into a capsule and
swallow that capsule religiously.  Would be nice if I knew *why* and
*what for* the C and B6 helped do.  But I guess most sources feel that
too much science will bore the reader.  Sigh.

Also, I'm curious about how many sources say that taking large doses
of B6, some say even over 200 mg per day, may result in nerve damage.
This was the case when many women heard that B6 will help with PMS, so
they took megadoses of it *without any other B-complex supplement*.
The symptoms are feelings of numbness in hands and feet, and a strange
duck-like walking gait.  Then the other day, I saw that some of the
possible symptoms of, say, a severe B5 and biotin (a B vitamin)
deficiency was, you guessed it, numbness in hands and feet, odd gait.
Given how it seems to be common knowledge that B vitamins are
eliminated as a source, it seems odd many people don't wonder if B6
overdose symptoms may be due to the fact that the elimination of B6
from the body causes severe deficiencies of the other B vitamins.  So
rather, many mainstream and more ``conservative'' sources would say
that B6 has possible severe side effects (*nerve damage*? I'll sure
say), and leave it at that.  Also, I've found that many of the most
conservative sources are often those from prestigious Harvard Medical
School or other type places.  The same type of statist AMA crowd who
love FDA regulations, and seem to enjoy people getting angry about the
fascist FDA ban on tryptophan.

A final rant on nootropils: I find it odd that many people seem to
think of nootropils as simple IQ increasers.  I certainly feel that
half of the cognitive battle is just keeping one's personal psychology
at a good enough level.  Now, c'mon, how many of us on this list feel
that we are utilizing ourselves to our absolute fullest advantage, and
that our maximal brainpower is the bottleneck in our performance?

No, quite the contrary, in my experience, most of the struggles of
life and intelligence are primarily *will*-oriented.  Think of the
many mundane everyday things that are the *real* bottlenecks in our
behavior.  Ask yourself which would help you more: a 10% increase in
short term memory, or a dose of willpower that can help you conquer a
bad habit, or help you fashion for yourself a stronger, more
integrated self?  Since some of the principles of Extropianism are
*self*-transformation, dynamic *optimism* -- these are heavily
reason-reality-oriented *psychological* factors.  This criticism is
the same that I have for many Objectivists, some of whom I've gotten
to know around MIT, who seem to feel that just talking about reason
and reality are sufficient, but don't appear to me to actually
*practice* their ``objective'' principles rigorously.  What good it is
to talk about 5-10% increases in IQ, the coming Singularity, the fine
points in Objectivist philosophy, the nth subtlety, if one is
embarassingly in entropic mental or physical circles of habits, which
*willpower*, and *not* eviscerated reason, will overcome?

So anyhow, my point is that many sources on nootropils seem to ignore
basic nutrition and the precursors of the basic 4 neurotransmitters in
our brain (choline/lecithin -> acetylcholine; L-phenylalanine/
L-tyrosine/L-dopa -> dopamine and norepinephrine; L-tryptophan ->
serotonin).  Often some cases of mental or psychological illness have
been linked with low levels of any of these 4 neurotransmitters.
Violently suicidal people with very low urine levels of a chemical
which results from serotonin breakdown.  Depression with low dopamine
and norepinephrine.  Alzheimer's with problems in the cholinergenic
part of the brain, thus acetylcholine.  All the piracetam or Hydergine
won't really help you if you're deficient in some basic
neurotransmitter.  Or how about taurine, a neuroregulator, which some
vegans may have deficiencies of (taurine doesn't seem to be as well
studied as some other amino acids), and an overdose of which might
slow down one's brain and nerves too much?  Or how about magnesium, an
overdose of which was injected to kill lab animals, before they found
out that many of the animals were probably dying by suffocation
(magnesium injections were then banned, of course, by our fascist
government).  But not getting enough magnesium is bad for one's
nerves, and can make one nervous, irritable, and so forth.  What about
chromium, which helps in a rather mysterious glucose tolerance factor,
a deficiency of which hurts one's ability to utilize insulin, which
could play havoc with one's blood glucose level, with dramatic effects
on cognition.  Chromium deficiency, several sources say, is widespread
in America.  Or how about poor blood, especially for many of us who
may be more of the engineer/scientist non-exercising poor-diet types.
Iron defiency, or rather, hemoglobin deficiency, is another problem.
A lot of people have less than optimal amounts of hemoglobin.  Want to
increase brainpower?  Try making sure that your brain is getting
enough oxygen to breathe and carry out its proper functions.

But no, people seem to be more interested in pure double-blind
cognition experiments with nootropils.  Statistics say that 80-90% of
Americans are deficient in some RDA, and as well all know,
deficiencies are simply to remedy and often provide quick and
immediate health benefits.  And this is only with the RDA, let alone
the many other vitamins and minerals whose defiencies can also
manifest deleterious effects.  And this is only the RDA as well, many
of whose ``proper dosages'' are frauds which were often adjusted
downwards when the government found that people's diets were poorer
than they ought to have been.  Why set a figure that your scientists
recommend as the *minimum* for good health, when over half of America
would fail that *minimum*?  And all this is aside from the fact that
most Americans eat a poor diet as a whole, meaning too much fat, too
much peroxidized foodstuffs and other carcinogens.

I'd be much happier if nootropil experiments were done with healthy
people who exercised, ate a good diet of mostly if not all grains,
fruits, and vegetables, led regular and non-erratic lifestyles.  I
would be much happier with results from *healthy* double-blind
experimental subjects were often engaged in daily productive mental
effort, rather than watching TV and gossiping all day.  I'm suspicious
that many nootropic effects may come from giving people a primarily
*psychological* lift, which then results in increased alertness and
performance, rather than pure cognitive increases.  (Note: this does
not contradict my previous statement that there isn't enough research
on safe primariliy *psychological* nootropils.  In fact, this is quite
my point: that often psychology and willpower matter much more than
actual brain capacity.  Likewise, I wonder if many nootropics simply
increased intelligence because the recipients were plain happier and
more alert.)

Finally, I have read from many sources about how a few certain fatty
acids are essential to good health, particularly alpha-linoleic and
linoleic acid.  They are used for many different things, and many good
nutrition books would give you the biochemical pathways for such fatty
acids.  The alpha-linoleic acid, linseed oil is the apparently a good
source, gets made into EPA and DHA, which are also found in fish oil
supplements.  It seems that the brain can use some of the EPA and DHA;
brain matter is particularly DHA rich.  Note that mother's milk
contains lots of alpha-linoleic and linoleic acid, whereas cow milk
contains virtually none, which seems to highlight all the more how
mother's milk is the best food for infants.  Given how newborns would
seem to need brain food builders immediately after birth, one wonders
at what babies might lose in total intelligence by not receiving any
dietary intake of the fatty acids required in the brain.  Also, it
really seems that fish is indeed quite a ``brain food,'' as the old
wives tale goes.  DMAE, choline, EPA, and DHA are all found in higher
quantities in various fish.

My problem once again is that many of these fatty acids appear to be
very polyunsaturated fats.  Do most cooking or fish oil manufacturers
take care to avoid rancidity in the products?  Again, I would dissolve
lots of ascorbyl palmitate in these oils just to be sure, but how can
I be sure the oils are safe before I buy them and take them home?

A final note.  Alpha-linoleic and linoleic acids are apparently
*essential* -- your body can't make them.  Furthermore, they play an
essential role in prostaglandin synthesis.  It seems that one of the
things one can do if one is concerned about cardiovascular health is
to note that of the three known prostaglandins which involve fatty
acid intake, one of them seems to make platelets stickier: PGI2, which
derives or is helped synthesized from arachnodic (sp?) acid, which is
found exclusively in animal products, whereas either PGI1 or PGI3 is
an anti-platelet sticking prostaglandin, and comes from the other
fatty acids, which are found in greater concentrations in plant and
fish products.  I don't have a chart of this in front of me right now;
I hope I have my PGI's and fatty acids correct.

T'would seem like almost a conspiracy considering the sheer number of
things that they find out ever year about the bad effects of meat.
Here's *yet another* reason to go easy on meat, beef and pork in
particular.

