Subject: DIET: fats and fatty acids

I felt that I may as well post some more on these subjects to give a
fuller context.  Besides, I am learning as I write (and making some
changes to my diet and supplementation accordingly).

Correction: as somebody pointed out to me, I used the term
``nootropil'' incorrectly.  Before I sent my last post, I was using
``nootropic,'' which is the proper usage, but then I chickened out and
used nootropil exclusively.  Mea culpa.

In my previous post, I noted that ``alpha-linoleic'' acid was one of
the two essential fatty acids from which other essentials fatty acids
can be made.  The name is actually ``alpha-linolenic'' (it's the
``n,'' stupid :).  Careless, mea culpa.  Also, supposedly ``Alena''
(Alpha linoLENic Acid) is a new coinage of this word.  If what some
sources say is correct, Alena deficiency might be the most widespread
deficiency in America.

This is 1/2 of the essential fatty acid path:

Alena -> EPA -> DHA

The other 1/2 is:

linoleic (note, not linoLENic) acid -> GLA (gamma-linoLENic acid) ->
Dihomogamma-linolenic acid -> arachidonic acid [this is the proper
spelling of the fatty acid said to be found primarily in animal
products]

The name of the prostaglandin that EPA (another fatty acid) helps to
stimulate production of is prostacyclin (I recall it being named PGI2
in another context), which helps to prevent the stickier kinds of
blood cells, hence helping keep the blood vessels free of dangerous
clots.  There was also one reference claiming that diet EPA helps cut
down on production of thromboxane, which is also a prostaglandin, but
one that induces blood cell clumping.  It seems that EPA and
arachidonic acid are at cross purposes when it comes to clot
formations.  So EPA helps against platelet aggregation and also thins
the blood as well: taking too much, as one enthusiastic Oxford
professor did when eating an almost all fish diet, resulted in the
Oxford professor developing anemia and dangerously prolonged bleeding
times.

Also, I found very interesting a study where lactating mothers taking
fish oil supplements, which contain EPA and DHA, had dramatic
increases in the DHA in their milk.  DHA is found heavily in the
brain, the retina.  Note that these polyunsaturated fats are very
easily oxidized, and can go dangerously rancid very quickly.  In fact,
there are some experiments which show that rats being fed diets with
polyunsaturated fats (uncertain whether they were being fed
unprocessed oils or processed oils, which have can be in the trans-
formation; this is very important; more below), that the higher the
level of polyunsatured fats, the more errors they made in maze tests.
Talk about neg-nootropic effects!  In fact, the fact that the brain is
so highly made of polyunsatured fats explains, for example, the
vitamin C pumps that keep brain levels of vitamin C far above blood
vitamin C levels, because the vitamin C is an anti-oxidant that
protects the easily peroxidized brain tissue.

Also, diets high in EPA and DHA can significantly reduce the amount of
bodily leukotrienes, which cause inflammation of tissue.  Some studies
on arthritis show noticeable improvement with fish oil supplements
with respect to placebo groups.  Some sources also cite that GLA,
found in evening primrose oil, may help arthritis as well.

The best source of linoleic and linolenic acids appears to be flax or
linseed oil.  These acids, particularly GLA, Alena, and linoleic acid,
form the top of the fatty acid chain I typed out above.  You know,
it's a shame that with many health supplements and foods, that there
will be people making irresponsible and often downright fraudulent
claims on a certain food, which then leads to a backlash from an
obsessive-compulsive cure-over-prevention medical establishment (by
the way, how many doctors do you see actually practicing what they
preach?; how many doctors aren't overweight?; hmm...).  For example,
the fascist FDA (I presume it's the FDA; who else has the jurisdiction
to ban foodstuffs?) at one point put restrictions upon linseed oil
because of all the claims its promoters were making.  But you can
purchase linseed oil now.  An interesting snippet I picked up was that
cooking with linseed oil can be pretty pungent, since it might smell a
lot like fish being fried.

One source says that one ought to get all one's EPA and DHA not from
fish oils or from fish, but rather from linseed or other plant oils,
because not only are plant oils cheaper, less easy to peroxidize, but
it may be better to let the body decide how much EPA and DHA it needs
and convert from Alena to EPA and DHA on its own.  Also, some people,
older people, it seems, can't convert linoleic acid to GLA well due to
a decline in the requisite enzyme delta-5-desaturase, so evening
primrose oil would be recommended as well.

Beware that the most essential fats are these polyunsatured fats,
which are heavily prone to oxidation.  Linseed oil, for example, needs
to be in black bottles (dark brown doesn't cut it) and needs to be
refrigerated to prevent oxidation, and bottles should have a pressing
date and four month expiration date.  Sheesh; you may as well put some
ascorbyl palmitate or BHA or BHT while you are at it, just to be safe.

A very important word of caution.  All sources have remarked upon how
the cis- form of fatty acids is what is found in nature, and that
trans- forms, which are produced when oils and fats are processed, are
dangerous and should be eliminated from the diet.  Thus, when one buys
these oils, one needs to make sure that they are cold-pressed and
unrefined.  Bleaching, deodorizing, hydrogenation, and heating, all
these will ruin the cis- form fats and turn them into the dangerous
trans- form.  I've heard too much about all the bad things of trans-
form polyunsatured fats.  For example, that they inhibit the liver's
ability to turn fats into other useful substances and fats, help raise
blood fat levels, and the fact that your body is building itself with
fats that are just plain unnatural and serve no biological purpose
except to interfere in normal bodily functions.

So ironically, margarine and other hydrogenated fats, such as
shortening, seem to do more harm than the animal saturated fats,
butter for example, that they were meant to replace.  A figure was
given that 60% of fats consumed in the American diet are hydrogenated.
Add to that the fact that Americans also eat too much saturated fats.
Saturated fats seem to do little than be burned for energy.  In other
words, dietary saturated fats seem to go primarily into right into
one's bodily fat stores; or rather, that a gram of saturated fat is
probably going to wind up as a gram of unwanted cellulose and beer
bellies.  There is other research, which I am less familiar with, that
has led many public sources to declaim against saturated fats because
they help cause heart disease.  The effects of saturated fats seem to
be as they are muchly because, as you can tell by now, polyunsatured
fats have many double bonds which are the reason for why they are so
easily oxidized as well as being used for the infinite biological
purposes in your body.  Saturated fats, on the contrary, saturate all
the double bonds with hydrogen.  Note that monosaturated means that
there is one double bond, and poly means, of course, many.  Finally,
your body seems to be able to make the saturated fats that it needs
(and it does need them to protect your organs and pad your skin, just
as in animals) from unsaturated fats.  Hence, saturated fats are
nonessential, and everywhere people say that one should eliminate or
radically cut down on one's intake of them.

Thus, the majority of fats in the American diet are either downright
toxic (hydrogenated fats) or plain fattening (saturated fats), both
types being contributors to clogged arteries and heart disease.  No
wonder it seems that many come to the conclusion that fat is all bad
-- because practically all sources of fat in the American diet are
bad.  Ironically, taking supplements of certain ``good'' fats will
help *undo* the life-endangering effects of the ``bad'' fats.  The
effects can be dramatic.  The whole interest in EPA began with
noticing that Eskimos ate little fiber (not too many plants growing up
there, ya know), lots of fat and protein, but still had low levels of
heart disease, and if anything a prolonged bleeding time.  By the
standard brain-dead ``common wisdom,'' those Eskimos should all be
dying in high proportions of heart disease.  But such is not the case.
More rants on my perceptions of massive public disinformation and
confusion on health matters later.

I'd like to just quote (without permission) from pages 86-87 of
Michael Colgan's _Optimum Sports Nutrition_, which can be recommended
for one no nonsense no hype scientific source of sports nutrition (and
what's good for an athlete's body is in general good for *your* body,
too; don't let the title mislead you, and you *do* intend to exercise
