Damn: breads, cookies and other baked goods, frozen dinners, peanut
butter, salad dressings, pies, and processed meats?  That's
practically all the sources of fats that I am currently consuming in
my diet.

Note that no nutrition/supplement book has ever been had all the fully
relevant information on nutrition.  *Every* book, it seems, has missed
something essential to a better understanding of human nutrition.  And
a comment on Pearson and Shaw: they seemed to have been missing the
boat on certain issues lately.  You would think that anybody with that
much money, public visibility, would do exhaustive research with all
the time and resources -- not to mention monetary benefits to be
gained thereby -- at their hands.  This goes for all the nutritional
experts and authors of the books I have been looking through.  Dean
Ornish, for example, has written some excellent books on a whole set
of healthy habits to help prevent heart disease, but he seems to be
missing some of the most interesting findings in recent nutritional
science, particularly regarding supplementation.

So the point is that nobody has ever done a thorough job, and
furthermore, all sources need to be qualified and taken with some
grains of salt.  In addition, many sources will cite sources, or
sources about sources, or hearsay, or hearsay from hearsay.  So, for
example as I am typing this, I brought 5 nutrition/supplement books in
front of me to this workstation, and I am flipping through and pulling
from each one as appropriate (which is why I'm not bothering to give
all the exact individual sources).  So it's difficult to know which
sources to trust.  Go by reputation and intuition, I guess.

