So, anyhow, hydrogenated and saturated fats are basically bad for you.
So thus the standards nutrition advice that all unsaturated
non-hydrogenated vegetable oils are basically safe, correct?  Nope!

Oxygen, heat, and light are what damage polyunsaturated seed oils, so
even the best available cold-pressed and unrefined seed oils are still
damaged a bit in the processing.  So the standard industrial scale,
high heat processed, refined, bleach and deodorized, oils are far
worse, with fats damaged enough that they can longer be useful in the
body, and become dangerous to one's health.

So I wonder if the knee-jerking anti-animal fats crowd realized this
when they crowed about how they pressured McDonalds into finally
replacing the fat for their french fries from using animal lard to
using vegetable oils, which have less saturated fats and cholesterol.
