So just to rant on the other rants in the health marketplace and
information centers.  Some sectors say that all fats are bad, and one
should cut down on *all* fats indiscriminantly.  Oh?  What about
certain essential fatty acids, some of which have pronounced
anti-clotting, anti-heart disease, and anti-inflammatory effects?  Or
how about the sectors that rather than advising cutting down on most
bad fats in genral, but instead first recommend substitute unsaturated
fats for saturated fats because of the cholesterol and other blood
lipid levels effects?  Well, gee, what about the dangerous trans- fats
from hydrogenation, or the peroxidation that can result from
unsaturated fats being exposed too much to heat, light, and oxygen?
Peroxidized fats are catalyze *free radicals reactions*, and thus are
not only far worse for your heart than saturated fats, but they will
do other damage as well, such as depressing your immune system,
causing cancer, cross-linking your tissues, and helping to deplete
your sources of bodily antioxidants.  It is true that cholesterol can
be traced to increases in blood concentrations of the killer.  But it
is also true, as Pearson and Shaw write in _Life Extension_, and many
other sources (I am reminded of _The 8-week Cholesterol Cure_), that
your body produces more cholesterol per day than in the average
cholesterol-heavy American diet.  So what gives?  It's obviously a
much more complex process.  Or how about the fact that high doses of
niacin might help cut down on blood cholesterol levels?  So given all
these fuzzy areas, it's really irresponsible of our so-called ``health
experts'' to say that either all fat is bad, or that unsaturated fats
are always better than saturated fats, without giving serious
qualifications to their advice.

